## ABSTRACT
Because of a weak interface-bonding force between metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) and substrates and the loss of customization in structural designs owing to the lack of the regulation of ion sites, MOFs tend to escape from the constructed composite template. In this study, the as-prepared 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidyl-1-oxyl (TEMPO)-oxidized algae cellulose nanofibers (TACFs) were used to chelate metal ions at controllable sites and subsequently firmly entangle the assembled MOF crystals. The distribution of ions and synthesized MOFs inside the gel was monitored using Raman imaging technology, which provided an intuitive approach for visually observing the ions and MOF distribution. Using this technology, the synthesized customizable TACFs@ZIF-07 aerogels exhibited a high specific surface area (734.7 m²/g), low density (6.19 mg/cm³), controlled particle distribution, good underwater structural stability, and excellent adsorption of dyes. This study provides a way for solving the dispersion problem of MOFs in nanofibrous aerogels using Raman imaging technology–assisted microcosmic fixed-point design.
## KEYWORDS
cellulose nanofiber, aerogel, Raman imaging, ion distribution, metal–organic framework
## 1 Introduction
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) are types of three-dimensional (3D) porous materials comprising metal ions or clusters and organic linkers regularly arranged at multiple scales. Owing to their specially designed periodic micro/mesoporous architectures, MOFs exhibit ultrahigh porosity and specific surface areas, facilitating their extensive use in gas storage [1, 2], separation [3], catalysis [4], drug delivery [5], and sensing [6]. MOF crystals are in the state of powder or colloidal crystals and lack flexibility and self-supporting characteristics, which result in poor processability and moldability, severely restricting their application fields. To address this problem, MOFs can be deposited on the interface of certain natural polymers (cellulose [7]), synthetic polymer materials (polyester [8]), flexible support materials (fiber [9], foam [10], and aerogel [11]), and nanoinorganic materials (metakaolin [12]) by coating [13], spraying [14], and layer-by-layer self-assembly methods [15], which considerably improve their processability. However, the weak interface-bonding force and the lack of customization in structural design between MOFs and substrate materials lead to an irregular aggregation of MOF crystals on the template, thereby reducing the specific area of composite materials and limiting their excellent performance [16].A new strategy was used for growing MOFs after a soft–soft interaction between metal ions and ligands (cellulose, polyimide, or chitin with abundant hydroxyl and amide groups) [17–19] in a hybrid material system to achieve improved interfacial stability between MOFs and substrate materials. This new strategy avoided the aggregation of MOFs and improved the adhesion between MOFs and substrate materials. Subsequently, various types of flexible composite films [20], aerogels [21], nanopaper sheets [22], and textile materials [23] with good formability were prepared. However, in the precedent MOF hybrid material system, the substrate materials rarely contributed to the adhesion of MOFs to the material via mechanical restrictions. Therefore, the MOFs easily fell off the support material, considerably reducing the material efficiency.Nanocellulose, particularly fiber-like cellulose nanofibrils with good flexibility and a high aspect ratio, can be derived from various sources, such as tunicates, plants, and bacteria, and has been used to design aerogel-based materials [24]. In particular, 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidyl-1-oxyl (TEMPO) oxidation technology can selectively oxidize C6 primary alcohols into the cellulose molecular chain to carboxyl groups to obtain a negatively charged cellulose nanofiber dispersion with good dispersibility and ionic thixotropy. Nanofibrous aerogels fabricated using TEMPO-oxidized cellulose nanofibers have attracted attention in catalyst carriers, tissue engineering, sensing, solvent absorbents, filtration, and templates [25]. However, studies on MOF composites prepared using TEMPO-oxidized nanocellulose as support materials usually focus on the morphology and loading of MOFs
A new strategy was used for growing MOFs after a soft–soft interaction between metal ions and ligands (cellulose, polyimide,
or chitin with abundant hydroxyl and amide groups) [17–19] in a hybrid material system to achieve improved interfacial stability between MOFs and substrate materials. This new strategy avoided the aggregation of MOFs and improved the adhesion between MOFs and substrate materials. Subsequently, various types of flexible composite films [20], aerogels [21], nanopaper sheets [22], and textile materials [23] with good formability were prepared. However, in the precedent MOF hybrid material system, the substrate materials rarely contributed to the adhesion of MOFs to the material via mechanical restrictions. Therefore, the MOFs easily fell off the support material, considerably reducing the material efficiency.
Nanocellulose, particularly fiber-like cellulose nanofibrils with good flexibility and a high aspect ratio, can be derived from various sources, such as tunicates, plants, and bacteria, and has been used to design aerogel-based materials [24]. In particular, 2,2,6,6-tetramethylpiperidyl-1-oxyl (TEMPO) oxidation technology can selectively oxidize C6 primary alcohols into the cellulose molecular chain to carboxyl groups to obtain a negatively charged cellulose nanofiber dispersion with good dispersibility and ionic thixotropy. Nanofibrous aerogels fabricated using TEMPOoxidized cellulose nanofibers have attracted attention in catalyst carriers, tissue engineering, sensing, solvent absorbents, filtration, and templates [25]. However, studies on MOF composites prepared using TEMPO-oxidized nanocellulose as support materials usually focus on the morphology and loading of MOFs
[25, 26], paying insufficient attention to the influence of the ion-induced gel process on MOF growth. As a research method for visually observing the distribution of groups in substances, Raman imaging technology has been used to identify physical and chemical crosslinking points [27]. Therefore, using 3D Raman technology for the in situ monitoring of the ion-induced gel process, the influence of the distribution of ion sites in the cellulose molecular chain on MOF growth can be clarified, which can endow MOF composite aerogels with a unique structure.
In this work, TEMPO-oxidized algae cellulose nanofibers (TACFs) with a high aspect ratio and a controllable content of carboxyl groups were extracted from algae residue and used to chelate the central ions to firmly entangle the assembled MOF crystals. Both the distribution of \( C\mathrm{o}^{2+} \) chelated on TACFs and the assembled ZIF-67 crystals were visually observed using Raman imaging technology. Results demonstrated that the carboxyl groups of TACFs could ensure uniformly distributed ion sites in the gel and considerably affected the growth capacity and size of ZIF-67. The uniformly distributed MOF crystals, tightly entangled by cellulose nanofibers in the aerogel, endowed the composite aerogel with a high specific surface area \( (734.7\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}) \), low density \( (6.18\ \mathrm{mg/cm}^{3}) \), good structural stability, good mechanical properties, and excellent adsorption of cationic dyes. This study develops a simple strategy for preparing sustainable and biofriendly adsorption materials, provides a reference for the in-depth understanding of the interaction between ions and substrate materials for constructing MOF-based aerogels, and offers useful applications of the materials.
## 2 Experimental
## 2.1 Materials
Mingyue Seaweed Group Co., Ltd (Qingdao, Shandong) provided brown algae residue, the waste obtained after extracting alginic acids from brown algae. TEMPO and 2-methylimidazole were purchased from Aladdin Biochemical Technology Co., Ltd (Shanghai, China). Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate, methanol, sodium hydroxide, and hydrochloric acid were purchased from Guangzhou Chemical Reagents (Guangzhou, China). All the reagents were used as received.
## 2.2 TACF preparation
First, the algae cellulose obtained from the algae residue was purified. For purification, the algae residue was crushed, dispersed in a \( 5\ \mathrm{wt.\%} \) NaOH solution, and stirred for \( 12\ \mathrm{~h~} \) at room temperature to remove protons. The substance was successively dispersed in \( 7\% \) (v/v) \( \mathrm{HCl} \) ( \( 12\ \mathrm{h} \), stirring, room temperature (RT)) and \( 4\% \) (v/v) \( \mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2} \) ( \( 6\ \mathrm{h} \), stirring, \( 80\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \) ) solutions to remove minerals and pigments. After washing thoroughly with deionized water and freeze-drying, a macroscopically white fluffy powder (algae cellulose) was obtained. Subsequently, \( 1\ \mathrm{g} \) algae cellulose was suspended in the aqueous solution and subjected to ultrasonic irradiation for \( 30\ \mathrm{min} \) before adding \( 0.016\ \mathrm{g} \) TEMPO and \( 0.1\ \mathrm{g} \) NaBr. The mixed solution \( (\mathrm{pH} = 10) \) comprising sodium hypochlorite \( (5\ \mathrm{mM}) \) and hydrochloric acid \( (0.1\ \mathrm{M}) \) solutions was added to the above system to trigger the TEMPO oxidization reaction. The \( \mathrm{pH} \) level of the mixture was constantly maintained at 10 by adding the \( \mathrm{NaOH} \) solution \( (0.5\ \mathrm{M}) \), and the mixture was stirred continuously until \( \mathrm{NaOH} \) depletion did not occur. Subsequently, the mixture was dialyzed in water to obtain TACFs.
## 2.3 TACFs@Co\(^{2+}\) gel preparation
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate solutions (11.4, 17.2, and \( 34.4\ \mathrm{mM} \)) and \( 0.98\ \mathrm{wt.\%} \) TACF suspensions were mixed in customized molds at a ratio of 1:3 for \( 10\ \mathrm{h} \). After gelation, the fabricated TACFs@Co\(^{2+}\) hydrogel was immersed in methanol to form a TACFs@Co\(^{2+}\) organogel via solvent replacement.
molds at a ratio of 1:3 for \(10\,\mathrm{~h~}\) . After gelation, the fabricated TACFs \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{C}0^{2+}\) hydrogel was immersed in methanol to form a TACFs \(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{C}0^{2+}\) organogel via solvent replacement.
## 2.4 TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogel preparation
TACFs@Co\(^{2+}\) hydrogels were immersed in a methanol solution of 2-methylimidazole (the solution concentration corresponds to the cobalt ion concentration, e.g., 45.6, 68.8, and \( 130\ \mathrm{mM} \)) for \( 6\ \mathrm{h} \). Thereafter, the solution was washed with methanol and ethanol in turn. Finally, the obtained TACFs@ZIF-67 gels were separated and subjected to supercritical \( \mathrm{CO}_{2} \) drying to fabricate the TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels.
