## 2.6 Characterization
TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels were subjected to scanning electron microscopy (SEM; Sigma 500, Zeiss, Germany) at an acceleration voltage of \( 5\ \mathrm{kV} \). The cellulose nanofibers were characterized via atomic force microscopy (AFM; Bruker Multimode 8, USA) using silicon nitride probes (SNL-10, Bruker). Transmission electron microscopy (TEM; JEM-2100, JEOL, Japan) was performed on TACFs at an acceleration voltage of \( 200\ \mathrm{kV} \). The thermal decomposition temperature of TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels was determined using a thermogravimetric (TGA) analysis system (Q500, TA, USA) at a heating rate of \( 10\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathrm{min} \) from 80 to 700 \( ^{\circ}\mathrm{C} \). The Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface areas were measured and calculated using nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms on an adsorption analyzer (ASAP 2020, Micromeritics, USA). 3D Raman imaging was performed using a laser confocal Raman imaging microscope (inVia, Renishaw, UK). The excitation wavelength of the laser was \( 785\ \mathrm{nm} \). Raman maps were obtained using a spatial resolution of \( 200\ \mathrm{nm} \). X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a Rigaku Ultima IV X-ray diffractometer with \( \mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha \) radiation \( (\lambda = 0.15418\ \mathrm{nm}) \). The powder was placed flat on the sample holder and scanned at a \( 2\theta \) angle of \( 5^{\circ}-50^{\circ} \) at a step speed of \( 3^{\circ}/\mathrm{min} \). Attenuated total reflectance-Fourier transform infrared (ATR-FTIR) measurements were conducted using a Nicolet IS50 FTIR spectrometer (USA). Mechanical tests were performed on the composite aerogels using an INSTRON instrument (model 3367). The cylindrical cellulose aerogels (height: \( 12\ \mathrm{mm} \) and diameter: \( 10\ \mathrm{mm} \)) were compressed at a speed of \( 1\ \mathrm{mm/min} \).
## 3 Results and discussion
## 3.1 Formation of TACFs@ZIF-67 composite aerogels
Brown algae (Fig. 1(a)) are marine resources with abundant reserves. We conducted a series of purification treatments, including water, acid, and alkaline washing and bleaching, on the remainder of the algae residue after the purification of alginate to obtain the nanofiber-aggregated structural algae cellulose (Fig. S1 in the ESM). After the TEMPO-mediated oxidation treatment and ultrasonic dispersion, negatively charged TACFs with good
dispersion stability were prepared (Fig. 1(b) and Fig. S2 in the ESM). Introducing the C6 carboxyl group endowed TACFs with good dispersion stability via electrostatic repulsion and facilitated TACF suspensions to chelate with counter ions via electrostatic attraction, showing ionic gelation behavior.
By chelating the TACF suspension with \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) in a customized mold, the metal ions ionically crosslinked the TACFs, showing a sol–gel transition and achieving molded red TACFs@Co2+ gels (Fig. 1(c)). After replacing the gel with a methanol solvent and immersing it in an organic ligand solution, ZIF-67 crystals assembled on the metal ion sites. A series of purple TACFs@ZIF-67 gels were fabricated after thorough washing with water (Fig. 1(d)). Using supercritical \(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\) drying, nanofiber-structured TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels with a low density \((6.18~\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3})\), flexibility, and moldability were obtained (Fig. 1(e)). Interestingly, the assembled ZIF-67 nanoparticles were firmly entangled with the cellulose nanofibers (Fig. 1(f)) owing to the high aspect ratio of TACFs and the controllable low content of carboxyl groups in cellulose nanofibers \((0.693\,\mathrm{mmol/g}\), Fig. S3 in the ESM). For comparison, we mixed the ZIF-67 particles in the TACF solution to fabricate the TACFs–ZIF-67 hybrid aerogels. These aerogels showed poor underwater stability (Fig. S4 in the ESM), and the ZIF-67 particles were aggregated and easily separated from the aerogel (Fig. S5 in the ESM). Alternatively, TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels exhibited better structural uniformity and stability than TACFs–ZIF-67 hybrid aerogels, confirmed via microstructure and image characterizations (Fig. S4 in the ESM).
## 3.2 Purification and morphology control of algae cellulose nanofibers
Considering that the morphology and carboxyl group content of TACFs can affect the ion site distribution and MOF crystal morphology, SEM, AFM, and TEM analyses were used to
characterize the morphological changes in algae cellulose nanofibers during the treatment process (Fig. 2). Figures 2(a) and 2(b) show that the algae residue was brown and contained rich nanofibrous cellulose and numerous impurities, including inorganic salts, proteins, and pigments. In particular, the cellulose nanofiber network and impurity particles aggregated and agglomerated, severely limiting the properties and applications of algae nanocellulose. After purification, the fabricated algae cellulose appeared as a macroscopically white and fluffy powder (Fig. 2(c)). However, because of the strong hydrogen bond interaction between the cellulose, the algae cellulose showed evident aggregation (Fig. 2(d)).
Because ultrasound is a method for dispersing nanocellulose via mechanical force, it was used to disperse the algae cellulose in water. The result demonstrated that ultrasound was conducive to dispersing nanofibers; however, the nanofibers still showed large fiber bundle aggregates with the maximum diameter of \(418~\mathrm{nm}\) (Figs. 2(e)–2(g)). Furthermore, they exhibited noticeable sedimentation after three days of standing. Therefore, a simple mechanical ultrasound method cannot avoid the aggregation of nanocellulose because of the hydrogen bond interaction. To obtain nanofibers with high dispersion stability, a TEMPO-mediated oxidation treatment was used to oxidize the hydroxyl group at the C6 position (Fig. 2(h)). The TEMPO-oxidized and ultrasonic-treated TACFs achieved excellent dispersion stability in water without sedimentation, even after one month of standing. Because of the introduction of carboxyl groups in the cellulose chains, the electrostatic interaction repelled cellulose nanofibers, affording a uniform nanoscale dispersion with an average nanofiber height of \(\sim13\;\mathrm{nm}\) and an aspect ratio of more than 397, confirmed by TEM and AFM (Figs. 2(i)–2(j) and Fig. S6 in the ESM). The aforementioned results indicate that the TEMPO oxidation and ultrasonic treatments on the algae cellulose can maintain the high aspect ratio of the algae cellulose nanofibers and
endow the nanofibers with superb water dispersion and stability, providing the possibility of constructing ionic gels and entangling MOFs.
3.3 Visual observation of the distribution of ion sites chelated on cellulose nanofibers
Raman imaging technology is a convenient and accurate method for identifying the changes and distribution of chemical groups or noncovalent interactions in the gel. It can explore the in situ gelation process between TACFs and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) via electrostatic interactions (Fig. 3). Raman spectroscopy was used to monitor changes in the vibration peak signals before and after chelating ions (Fig. 3(a)). The asymmetric stretching vibration peak intensity of the cellulose at \(1,098~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) corresponding to the glycosidic bond was maintained after gelation, providing a clear Raman signal to determine the cellulose distribution (green, Fig. 3(b)) [28]. In particular, after \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) adsorption, the peak intensity at \(1,335~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) decreased considerably to approximately half the initial intensity (Fig. 3(b)) because the TACFs adsorbed \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) onto its –COOH functional groups, affecting the \(\mathrm{C}=\mathrm{O}\) vibration [29]. Therefore, by analyzing the peak intensity changes
at \(1,335~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), the interaction site of TACFs and \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) could be identified (red or orange, Fig. 3(c)).
Figure 3(d) shows a series of representative Raman spectra from points i–iii (marked in Fig. 3(c)), where the crosslinking point (ii) between the cobalt ion and TACFs was achieved owing to the drastic decrease in the peak intensity at \(1,335~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). By considering the intensity of the aforementioned Raman spectra as the top view (Fig. 3(e)), we can clearly observe the distribution of the cobalt ions, which were chelated between the cellulose molecular chains via electrostatic interactions. Moreover, we used 3D Raman imaging technology to visualize the process in which \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) began penetrating the TACF suspension, gradually leading to the gelation of the TACF solution. The results showed that the permeation behavior of cobalt ions was time-dependent, and cobalt ions would penetrate the gel in \(\sim10\,\mathrm{h}\) (Fig. S7 in the ESM). The controllable and uniform dispersion of metal ions provided the possibility for the growth and controllable distribution of ZIF-67 crystals. By the in situ monitoring of the chelation process between TACFs and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\), the distribution of ions as active sites was observed in TACFs@Co\(^{2+}\) gels, guiding the loading and synthesis of MOFs.
## 3.4 Regulation of the assembly of ZIF-67 crystals in TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels
As shown in Fig. 4, the controllable and uniform dispersion of metal ions indeed provided the possibility for the growth and controllable distribution of ZIF-67 crystals. Figure 4(a) shows that the organic ligand, 2-methylimidazole, was connected to the inorganic cobalt ions through self-assembly to form underwater-stable purple TACFs@ZIF-67 hydrogels with crystalline ZIF-67 crystals. Subsequently, by comparing the Raman spectrum of the ZIF-67 powder with that of the composite aerogel, we confirmed the characteristic peak signal \((676~\mathrm{cm^{-1}})\) [30] of ZIF-67 in the TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogel (Fig. 4(b)). XRD was used to study changes in the crystal structure of the algae cellulose during the preparation of composite aerogels, further confirming the synthesis of the ZIF-67 crystal in the aerogel (Fig. 4(c)(c)). The algae cellulose showed a typical type I structure.
Furthermore, the \(2\theta\) angle of \(14.8^{\circ}\), \(16.3^{\circ}\), and \(22.6^{\circ}\) were attributed to the (1i0), (100), and (200) crystal planes, respectively [31]. After TEMPO-mediated oxidation treatment, TACFs still showed the type I cellulose structure. Furthermore, after comparison with the crystal structure of standard ZIF-67, the \(2\theta\) angles of the composite aerogel at \(7.4^{\circ}\), \(12.8^{\circ}\), \(14.8^{\circ}\), \(16.5^{\circ}\), and \(18.1^{\circ}\) corresponded to the (110), (200), (211), (310), and (222) crystal planes of the ZIF-67 crystal [32], respectively, demonstrating the effective assembly of ZIF-67 crystals. Therefore, by analyzing the peak intensity at \(676\;\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), the distribution of the ion sites of ZIF-67 crystals in the aerogel could be identified (purple, Figs. 4(d) and 4(e)). Interestingly, the distribution of ZIF-67 crystals in the aerogel showed similar patterns using Raman imaging and SEM techniques (Fig. 4(e)), thus confirming the feasibility of using Raman imaging technology to visually observe the distribution of ion sites and guide the loading of ZIF-67 crystals.
Subsequently, we investigated the influence of ion concentration on the morphology of the TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels (Fig. 4(f)). Figure 4(f) shows that the ZIF-67 crystals supported by the composite aerogel gradually became denser as the central ion concentration increased, with an increase in the average particle size of ZIF-67 crystals from \(305~\mathrm{nm}\) at \(11.4~\mathrm{mM}\) to \(424.4~\mathrm{nm}\) at \(17.2~\mathrm{mM}\) and \(605.8~\mathrm{nm}\) at \(34.4~\mathrm{mM}\). Interestingly, the magnified SEM micrograph of the characteristic area showed that TACFs entangled the assembled MOF crystals because of the controllable ion site design. These characteristics indicate strong interactions between TACFs and ZIF-67 crystals, promoting the tight entanglement of ZIF-67 with TACFs.
In summary, by monitoring of the chelation process between TACFs and \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\), the distribution of ions as active sites was visualized in the TACFs@Co2+ gel and the loading of MOFs can be guided. Because of the high aspect ratio, easy aggregation, and entanglement of the nanofibers, ZIF-67 crystals with \(\mathrm{Co}(\mathrm{II})\) as the nucleus can be entangled in the nanofibers to form uniformly dispersed and structurally stable TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels.
## 3.5 Evaluation of the physical properties and adsorption performance of TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogels
Because of the uniform dispersion of the ZIF-67 crystals in aerogels, high specific surface area of MOF crystals, and tight entanglement of ZIF-67 with TACFs, the TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogel showed its feasibility as an adsorption material. First, the loading of MOFs in the TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogel fabricated using three ion concentrations was quantitatively determined using TGA analysis (Fig. S8 in the ESM) by analyzing the residual mass of the pristine ZIF-67 powder and composite aerogels at \(700\,^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\). The calculated contents of ZIF-67 in the MOF aerogels were \(17.7\,\mathrm{\wt.\%}\), \(33.2\,\mathrm{\wt.\%}\), and \(51.0\,\mathrm{wt.\%}\).
Subsequently, the TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogel with three loadings namely, TACFs@18% ZIF-67, TACFs@33% ZIF-67, and
TACFs@51% ZIF-67 aerogels) were characterized using nitrogen adsorption–desorption isotherms (Figs. 5(a)–5(c)). The adsorption isotherm revealed that the pristine cellulose aerogel exhibited a lower adsorption capacity than the composite aerogel loaded with MOF crystals in the low-pressure zone. Therefore, the loading of MOF crystals considerably improved the adsorption volume of the aerogel (Fig. 5(a)). Furthermore, the pore-wide distribution curve showed that abundant micropores (0.85–1.8 nm) appeared in the composite aerogel after loading the MOF crystals (Fig. 5(b)) and the assembled MOFs did not block the mesopores of the cellulose aerogel. Based on the BET calculation, the specific surface area of the TACFs@ZIF-67 aerogel with a hierarchical porous structure reached \(734.7\,\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), which is 2.56 times higher than that of the cellulose aerogel (Fig. 5(c)). These results indicated that a controllable distribution of ion sites for the fabrication of composite aerogels with both micropores and mesopores can considerably increase the specific surface area of the cellulose aerogel [33]. Additionally, the assembled MOFs further improved the mechanical properties of the composite aerogel, enhancing its fracture strength from 0.54 to \(1.26\,\mathrm{MPa}\) (Fig. S9 in the ESM). These findings indicate that the introduction of MOFs substantially improved the mechanical properties of the composite aerogels.
To evaluate the effect of ZIF-67 on the aerogels, adsorption capacities were calculated. The equilibrium adsorption capacities \((\mathrm{A}_{0})\) of MB and RhB were \(\sim\,58\) and \(\sim\,36\,\mathrm{mg/g}\), respectively
(Fig. 5(e)). Furthermore, the adsorption capacity of TACFs@ZIF-67 composite aerogel in this study was superior to those reported in other studies (Fig. 5(f)) [21, 35–40]. Thus, the strategy for fabricating MOF-based aerogels based on an in-depth understanding of the interaction between ions and substrate materials is critical for improving the function of materials. The distribution coefficient \((K_{\mathrm{D}})\) of RhB was \({\sim}28,\!000~\mathrm{{\mL/g}}\) at an equilibrium concentration \((K_{\mathrm{D}})\) of \(3~\mathrm{mg/L}\) based on the equation \(K_{\mathrm{D}}=q_{\mathrm{e}}/C_{\mathrm{e}}\) . To investigate the adsorption process, MB was used as the target dye and the TACFs@33% ZIF-67 aerogel functioned as the adsorbent. The thermodynamic behavior of the aerogel increased significantly with temperature (Figs. S13(a) and S14 in the ESM) because of the weakened adsorptive force between the dye and the aerogel at higher temperatures [41]. The standard enthalpy change \((\Delta H^{\circ})\) , Gibbs free energy change \((\Delta G^{\circ})\) , and entropy change \((\Delta S^{\circ})\) were estimated from Fig. S13(b) in the ESM using the following equations
\[
\ln K_{\mathrm{D}}=\frac{\Delta S^{\circ}}{R}-\frac{\Delta H^{\circ}}{RT}
\]
and
\[
\Delta G^{\circ}=-RT\ln K_{\mathrm{D}}
\]
where \(R\) is the universal gas constant \((8.314\;\mathrm{J}/(\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}))\) and \(T\) is the temperature (K). The \(\Delta G\) and \(\Delta H\) values were \(\sim-6.0\) and \(-9.6\) \(\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) , respectively, indicating a spontaneous and exothermic
adsorption process. Furthermore, the \(\Delta H^{\circ}\) value was below 40 \(\mathrm{kJ/mol}\) , indicating that physical adsorption dominated the process. The orderly arrangement of MB on the aerogel surface induced a negative \(\Delta S\) value \((-12.5\;\mathrm{J}/(\mathrm{mol}{\cdot}\mathrm{K}))\) [41]. The adsorption kinetics (Figs. S13–S15 in ESM) and isotherm (Fig. S16 in the ESM) linear fits demonstrated that MB adsorption conformed to the pseudo-second-order kinetic (Fig. S13(c) in the ESM) and Freundlich isotherm (Fig. S13(d) in the ESM) model based on the \(R^{2}\) values (Fig. S16 in the ESM).
The intraparticle diffusion modeling (Fig. 5(g)) revealed that the adsorption process involved three steps [42]. The dye molecules diffused on the aerogel surface and adsorbed on the active sites of the aerogel, finally entering the equilibrium stage. The \(\mathrm{pH}\) value at the point of zero charge \((\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{zpc}})\) of the aerogel and the \(\mathrm{pKa}\) value (4.52) of MB were used to determine the influence of the solution \(\mathrm{pH}\) on adsorption. The \(\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{zpc}}\) value of the TACFs@33% ZIF-67 aerogel was 8.4 (Fig. 5(h)), measured using the \(\mathrm{pH}\) drift method. When the solution \(\mathrm{pH}\) was far below \(\mathrm{pKa}\), the \(\mathrm{H^{+}}\) in the solution interacted with the negative dye, hindering its adsorption on the aerogel surface. Furthermore, the adsorption capacity decreased slightly from \(\mathrm{pKa}\) to \(\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{zpc}}\) because of the repulsive interaction between the positive aerogel and positive dye. When the \(\mathrm{pH}\) values increased to above \(\mathrm{pH}_{\mathrm{zpc}}\) the adsorption performance was positively related to the solution \(\mathrm{pH}\), attributed to the attraction between the negative adsorbent and positive MB dye. The results demonstrate that the electrostatic interaction was the dominant
force during MB adsorption on the aerogel. Furthermore, the slight shift in the ATR-FTIR peaks (Fig. 5(i)) at \(\sim\,1{,}417\) and \(\sim\) \(1{,}600~\,\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) before and after the adsorption indicate hydrogen bonds and electrostatic forces [43].
## 4 Conclusions
In summary, TACFs@ZIF-67 composite aerogels were fabricated by regulating the distribution of the coordination ion \(\mathrm{Co}^{2+}\) as reactive sites for assembling ZIF-67 crystals. TACFs, extracted from algae residue, exhibited a moderate content of the carboxyl groups, which endowed TACFs with ion-induced gelation behavior and ensured the uniform distribution of ion sites in the gel, confirmed using 3D Raman imaging technology. Furthermore, cellulose nanofibers in the aerogel tightly enlarged the uniformly distributed MOF crystals, relying on the ion sites, which endowed the composite aerogel with the advantages of a high specific surface area \((734.7~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g})\), low density \((6.18~\mathrm{mg/cm}^{3})\), structural stability, and excellent adsorption of cationic dyes. This study demonstrated a facile strategy for fabricating high-performance MOF aerogel-based adsorption materials based on an in-depth understanding of the interaction between ions and substrate materials.
